WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.180
Narrator: At first
glance, a fire ant hill -

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:06.640
or mound, as it's properly
called - looks impossibly small.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.640
And yet a colony of up to
250,000 ants call it home.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.730
But here's the secret: that mound

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.930
is just the tip of an enormous iceberg.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.030
So let's take a closer look
at what's inside an anthill.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.490
The mound is really the top

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.830
of an enormous underground
structure: the nest.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.850
Which is basically a giant
nursery: a nice, cozy place

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.290
to raise babies. A lot of babies.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:35.780
Their mother, the queen,
roams around the nest

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.490
while laying 1,500 eggs a day!

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.640
Now, all those baby ants need to live

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.960
in a narrow temperature range to grow.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.320
So that nest sports
temperature-controlled rooms.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.850
And it does so without
the help of an AC unit.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.770
The secret's in the design.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.810
The nest is arranged
like an ice cream cone.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.730
At the top, you have the mound
- the ice cream, as it were.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.200
Because it's above the surface,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.580
it warms up from the heat of the sun,

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.410
so the babies can snuggle
up in toasty chambers

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.560
networked throughout the mound.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.310
But they can't stay there all day,

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.140
or they'd get too hot.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:15.270
That's where the cone part of
the ice cream cone comes in.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.130
The mound is connected to
several vertical shafts

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:21.170
that plunge up to two
meters beneath the ground.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.290
That's taller than most humans.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.080
Throughout the day, adult
ants ferry the babies

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.620
up and down the shafts, chasing
that perfect temperature

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.270
for their young charges.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:34.340
The nest also sports
dozens of tapering tunnels

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.070
that branch off from these main shafts.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.560
These connect to small chambers
where the ants rest, eat,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.670
and feed the babies until it's time

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.480
to move the little ones once again.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.920
Now, there's one more type
of tunnel inside the nest,

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.670
but only a few ants ever use it.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.480
You see, someone needs to find food

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:53.750
for the rest of the colony,

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.350
but running around outside the
nest is dangerous business.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.530
That's where forager tunnels come in.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.500
These are a couple of horizontal passages

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.230
buried just a few
centimeters from the surface.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.440
But they run throughout
the entire territory,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.880
which can cover up to 185
square meters of land.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.880
By scurrying through these passageways,

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.260
the scouts can stay underground
as long as possible.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.120
But unfortunately, the
nest and all its roads

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.740
can't protect the ants from every threat.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.650
It turns out all sorts of critters

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.260
sneak inside fire ant nests.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.740
And while many of them
are actually harmless,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.330
others are horrible houseguests.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.110
For example, beetles burrow into the nest

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.140
and devour the eggs and larvae.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.110
But invaders aren't the
only threat to the colony.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.120
Occasionally, clueless humans

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.560
or major floods disturb the nest.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.520
And when that happens,

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.700
the fire ants have only one option, leave.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.700
Once a year on average,
the colony will move out

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.440
and build an entirely
new nest from scratch.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.550
And best of all, they only
need a few days to do it.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:03.920
That's right. Practically overnight,

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.890
meters upon meters of tunnels
can pop up in your yard.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.593
And all you'll notice is a tiny mound.

